research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-localization-bps.md:50:| `def-torus-equivariant-cohomology-cartan-model` | definition | For a compact torus `T` acting smoothly on a manifold `M`, define the Borel space `ET×_T M`, `H_T^*(M;R)`, the coefficient ring `H_T^*(pt;R)=Sym(t^*)` with degree-two generators, and the Cartan complex `(Sym(t^*)⊗Ω(M))^T` with `d_T=d-Σu_i ι_{X_i}`. Prove `d_T^2=0` on invariants. If using the Cartan model as a computational substitute, prove/cite the comparison for the declared compact-connected group and coefficients; do not conflate rational Borel cohomology, real Cartan cohomology, equivariant Chow groups and quotient-stack Borel–Moore homology. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-localization-bps.md:51:| `thm-borel-localization-for-compact-torus-actions` | theorem | For a compact smooth `T`-manifold and rational or real coefficients, define the multiplicative set generated by nonzero characters of `T`. Prove restriction to the fixed locus `M^T` becomes an isomorphism after that localization, with its precise compactness/finite-type hypotheses. The proof needs the fixed-set submanifold, a `T`-invariant tubular neighborhood, the equivariant Thom/Gysin map and the fact that each normal weight is invertible in the localized coefficient ring. On the complement, give a finite orbit-type/slice cover; each proper isotropy subgroup has a nonzero character vanishing on its identity component, so its equivariant cohomology is torsion after localization. State separately any properness/compact-support version for noncompact spaces. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-localization-bps.md:52:| `thm-atiyah-bott-berline-vergne-integration-formula` | theorem | Under a compact oriented smooth `T`-manifold hypothesis and an equivariant orientation of each fixed component's normal bundle, prove `∫_M α = Σ_{F⊂M^T} ∫_F i_F^*α/e_T(ν_F)` in the localized coefficient ring. Define `i_F`, equivariant pushforward, the normal Euler class, integration and degree conventions. Derive the isolated-fixed-point specialization from the fixed-locus theorem; specify the complex orientation/sign and the torus weights. The local proof should use `i_F^*i_{F*}(β)=e_T(ν_F)β` and the localized inverse restriction, rather than invoke a path-integral saddle. It is not a theorem for arbitrary noncompact instanton spaces or for an infinite-dimensional field space. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-localization-bps.md-53-| `def-equivariant-elliptic-complex-index` | definition | For a compact group `G` acting on a closed manifold and a `G`-equivariant elliptic complex, define the finite-dimensional kernel/cokernel representations and `Ind_G(D)∈R(G)`; define its character and the fixed-element hypothesis. Separate elliptic from transversally elliptic operators and say when the latter have infinite representation sums. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-localization-bps.md-54-| `thm-equivariant-index-character-formula` | theorem | Supply the fixed-set character formula for the precisely chosen elliptic/transversely elliptic class. Prove that the action preserves the symbol and all coefficient bundles, derive the fixed-locus contribution with its normal denominator and characteristic classes, and specify evaluation away from singular group elements. `thm-geometric-clifford-module-dirac-index-formula` is only the ordinary index and cannot discharge this item. Before using it as a load-bearing supplier, resolve the current status discrepancy recorded in the table above. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-localization-bps.md-55-| `def-finite-dimensional-q-exact-localization-data` | definition | Define a finite-dimensional graded integral (including its Berezin variables if used), integration cycle, odd differential/vector field `Q`, square `Q^2`, measure, action, observable and admissible deformation. Require a `Q`-invariant cycle/measure, an explicit domain/contour, and either compact support or stated decay ensuring no boundary terms and differentiation under the integral. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-localization-bps.md-56-| `thm-finite-dimensional-q-exact-deformation-invariance` | theorem | For the preceding data let `Q` be odd, `V` odd, `S` and the observable `O` even, require `Q^2V=0` (or that `Q^2` acts trivially on the integration cycle/integrand), `QS=QO=0`, and a `Q`-invariant measure and cycle (`div_μ Q=0` in coordinates). Prove `dZ_t/dt=0` by differentiating `∫O e^{-S-tQV}` and integrating the resulting `Q`-exact divergence. State a uniform integrable majorant for the derivative on every compact `t`-interval so differentiation/interchange is justified, and prove the boundary term vanishes (compact support or explicit decay). A large-`t` saddle limit additionally requires a proper coercive bosonic term, a characterized zero set, nondegenerate normal Hessian or a specified degenerate analysis, treatment of zero modes and a justified asymptotic limit. The finite-dimensional theorem does not establish a continuum Ward identity. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-localization-bps.md:57:| `def-framed-u-n-instanton-moduli-and-adhm-data` | definition | Fix Euclidean `R^4` or its one-point compactification, orientation/Hodge star, a framed principal `U(N)` bundle, the trace normalization and instanton number, the gauge group at infinity, and the ASD/SD sign. Define the ADHM vector spaces/maps `(B_1,B_2,I,J)`, moment-map equations, stability condition, `U(k)` quotient, torus action and any nonzero FI/noncommutative resolution parameter. Distinguish the smooth resolved space from the singular small-instanton boundary and the Uhlenbeck/Gieseker compactifications. |
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-2026-10-23/research-localization-bps.md:58:| `thm-adhm-fixed-point-instanton-count` | theorem | Prove the ADHM correspondence under the stated group, framing, stability and FI hypotheses; identify the fixed loci for `T^2×T_G`; prove the classification by `N`-tuples of partitions and compute the tangent/virtual-character weights. Apply the local finite-dimensional localization theorem only after specifying a legitimate equivariant pushforward or a compactification/proper fixed-locus condition. State whether the coefficient is a formal series in `q` or an analytic function. |
