research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/borel-index-reconciliation/before/items/ex-adhm-u1-one-box-fixed-point.md-45-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/borel-index-reconciliation/before/items/ex-adhm-u1-one-box-fixed-point.md-46-1.1 The chosen datum has $IJ=0$, so the complex moment-map equation holds. Since $I=1$ maps $W$ onto $V$, its image already spans $V$, so the datum is stable. The $GL(V)=\mathbb C^\times$ action removes the nonzero scalar choice of $I$; the quotient coordinates are $B_1,B_2$. [given, F1, algebra]
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/borel-index-reconciliation/before/items/ex-adhm-u1-one-box-fixed-point.md-47-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/borel-index-reconciliation/before/items/ex-adhm-u1-one-box-fixed-point.md-48-2.1 There is exactly one partition of $k=1$ for $N=1$, namely $Y_1=\{(0,0)\}$, so [F2] gives the unique fixed point. Here $\mathbf V_{\vec Y}=e_1$ and $\mathbf W=e_1^{-1}$, whence $\mathbf V^\vee\mathbf V=1$, $\mathbf V^\vee\mathbf W^\vee=1$, and $t_1t_2\mathbf W\mathbf V=t_1t_2$. Substitution in the tangent character gives $t_1+t_2$; the two additive weights are $\epsilon_1,\epsilon_2$, so the Euler class is $\epsilon_1\epsilon_2$. [given, F2, step 1.1, algebra]
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/borel-index-reconciliation/before/items/ex-adhm-u1-one-box-fixed-point.md-49-
research/advanced-qft-supersymmetry-build-2026-10-23-owner-resolution/borel-index-reconciliation/before/items/ex-adhm-u1-one-box-fixed-point.md-50-3.1 The degree-one coefficient is the sum over the single fixed point, so it equals $(\epsilon_1\epsilon_2)^{-1}$. This is a formal equivariant fixed-point contribution, not an asserted continuum path-integral value. [given, F2, step 2.1, algebra] ∎
--
